\title{Switches \code{\verb~|lazyeval~} and \code{\verb~|same~} in conversion}
\taxon{Change}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{changelog}
\tag{machine}
\tag{unreleased}
\tag{performance}
\meta{release}{Unreleased}
\meta{commit}{49fff61}
\import{base-macros}

\p{\code{\verb~|lazyeval~} makes a one-sided lazy-delta step against a side in weak head normal form one normalisation, where it was an unfolding followed by a cheap core step per round; it changes order only. On top of \code{\verb~|code,casetrees~} it took Init from 6.35 to 5.88 s and Std from 12.29 to 11.47 s CPU. \code{\verb~|same~} is the official structural identity test at the start of a conversion: a bounded walk of two values (256 nodes, 40 levels) with no judgment and no type. In its batch it took the Cedar slice from 2.86 s and 40.7 G instructions to 2.48 s and 38.4 G together with \code{\verb~|lazyeval~} (-13.1 \% CPU), and it makes the machine accept \code{\verb~|perf/loop-unfolded-once~}, which had cost 332 M ticks and a memory decline, in 266 k ticks. Both were published off, \code{\verb~|lazyeval~} with the fix of the conversion shortcuts (\commit{51fde1b}) and \code{\verb~|same~} in three versions during the day (\commit{49fff61}, \commit{605b9ab}, \commit{6520b42}), and both joined the default set at the integration of wave 6.}

\p{Detail: \src{rlvm-machine/SWITCHES.md}, \src{rlvm-machine/NOTES-M4.md}. Commit \commit{49fff61}. Session: [machine speed and pipeline](ses-CVTE).}
